SOLO-theme (PR Omar merges into Learrnsimply/edublink-child). Add to functions.php a frontend-only author-archive block. Place it right after the existing rest_endpoints filter (after line 27) so the two enumeration vectors (REST + author archive) live together:

/**
 * Block author archive pages (/author/{slug}/) — prevents username (login slug)
 * enumeration. Pairs with the rest_endpoints filter above (BUG-004/BUG-017).
 * Also stop WP from mapping ?author=N to a redirect that leaks the slug.
 */
add_action('template_redirect', function () {
    if (is_admin()) {
        return;
    }
    // Catch both pretty author archives and the ?author=N numeric probe.
    if (is_author() || (isset($_GET['author']) && (int) $_GET['author'] > 0)) {
        wp_safe_redirect(home_url('/'), 301);
        exit;
    }
}, 1); // priority 1: runs after the theme's own template_redirect at priority 0 (functions.php:1453) so custom-template logic is unaffected, but still early enough to short-circuit author rendering.

Notes / corrections to the original sketch:
1. The sketch missed the ?author=N numeric form. Even with pretty permalinks, /?author=1 makes WP issue a canonical 301 to /author/{slug}/, leaking the slug in the Location header BEFORE any is_author() template fires. The added $_GET['author'] guard closes that. (Defense-in-depth: optionally also add `add_filter('redirect_canonical', function($r,$req){ return (is_string($req) && strpos($req,'author=')!==false) ? false : $r; }, 10, 2);` to suppress that canonical redirect — but the template_redirect block alone already short-circuits it since it runs and exit()s.)
2. Use is_admin() guard (not a broad bail) so wp-admin author filters keep working; do NOT guard on is_user_logged_in() — the leak must be closed for anonymous visitors who may be logged-in too.
3. Use wp_safe_redirect (not wp_redirect) + explicit exit; 301 is correct for a permanent block.
4. Do NOT additionally unset rewrite rules / flush — that requires a flush_rewrite_rules() lifecycle and risks breaking other routes on a shared host; the template_redirect approach is zero-flush and reversible. This is the lower-risk fix for this theme.
Verify live after merge+deploy: `curl -sI 'https://learrnsimply.com/?author=1'` and `curl -sI 'https://learrnsimply.com/author/ahmedadel123422/'` should both return 301 to home (Location: https://learrnsimply.com/) with no author slug exposed.
